in @prism
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/math/bundle

# @epistemologic/math/lawvere — autopoietic closure as a Lawvere fixed point.
#
# The fixed-point theorem (Lawvere 1969, "Diagonal Arguments and Cartesian
# Closed Categories", Lecture Notes in Mathematics 92, 134-145): if there
# exists a point-surjective map X → X^X, then X has the fixed-point property
# — every endomap of X has a fixed point. The diagonal argument re-stated
# constructively. Russell's paradox, Cantor's theorem, Gödel's incompleteness,
# Tarski's undefinability, the Y combinator: all instances of one structural
# move. The grammar names that move.
#
# The autopoiesis bridge (Soto-Andrade & Varela 1984, "Self-reference and
# fixed points: a discussion and an extension of Lawvere's theorem." Acta
# Applicandae Mathematicae 2:1, 1-19. DOI 10.1007/BF00046985): autopoiesis
# IS the Lawvere fixed-point condition applied to the self-production map.
# A system is autopoietic iff its tick → tick map has a Lawvere fixed point.
# The system reproduces the conditions of its own reproduction. The closure
# is structural, not behavioral.
#
# Why this grammar exists now: the bundle tower's Closure level
# (@epistemologic/math/bundle, level 4) is currently abstract with a `\`
# verifier. Mirror's @cogito.reflect loop, @hash/coincidence's dark fallback
# (λ₀), kintsugi's settlement criterion, and the Refract operation all
# converge on the same structural object: the self-referential ground state
# of a self-producing process. This grammar gives that object a verifier.
#
# Worked example, untyped lambda calculus: the Y combinator
#   Y = λf. (λx. f (x x)) (λx. f (x x))
# has the property Y f = f (Y f) for every f. Y is the Lawvere fixed-point
# operator for the term algebra: every endomap f on the (untyped) term set
# has a fixed point Y f. The diagonal step IS the self-application (x x).
#
# Worked example, mirror's compiler: `mirror compile` reads a grammar,
# emits a crystal. `mirror compile` IS a grammar. Therefore there is a
# crystal of `mirror compile`. The fixed point of the compile-the-compiler
# loop IS the bootstrap crystal mirror is compiling toward. The loop
# terminates iff a Lawvere fixed point exists; mirror's self-hosting is
# the constructive proof that one does.
#
# Worked example, the @cogito tick: observe |> strategy |> perturb returns
# a new eigenboard. Applying the tick to the new eigenboard returns another.
# The loop terminates when the section reaches a fixed point of this map
# — when one more tick produces the section that went in. That is the
# Lawvere fixed point of the kintsugi reduction. Autopoiesis at the level
# of the compiler: the system reproduces the conditions of its own
# reproduction by closing its own holes.
#
# References:
#   Lawvere, F. W. (1969). "Diagonal arguments and Cartesian closed
#     categories." Lecture Notes in Mathematics 92, 134-145.
#   Soto-Andrade, J. & Varela, F. (1984). "Self-reference and fixed
#     points: a discussion and an extension of Lawvere's theorem."
#     Acta Applicandae Mathematicae 2:1, 1-19.
#     DOI 10.1007/BF00046985.
#   Maturana, H. & Varela, F. (1980). Autopoiesis and Cognition: The
#     Realization of the Living. D. Reidel.
#   Yanofsky, N. (2003). "A Universal Approach to Self-Referential
#     Paradoxes, Incompleteness and Fixed Points." Bull. Symbolic Logic
#     9(3), 362-386. arXiv:math/0305282.
#   Survey: "A Survey on Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem." arXiv:2503.13536.
#
# Spec: docs/specs/lawvere-grammar.md
#
# Cross-realisation in prism/core/src/bundle.rs (post-audit, 2026-05-21):
#   prism_core::LawvereFixedPoint IS the Rust-side realisation of this
#   grammar's fixed_point. The trait carries:
#     - is_idempotent_under(endomap) — the f(f(x)) == f(x) law.
#     - in_kernel()                  — ker(D) membership for D the
#                                       bundle's connection-induced
#                                       operator.
#   prism_core::bundle::Closure::Fixed is constrained to implement
#   LawvereFixedPoint, linking the bundle tower's level-4 closure to
#   this grammar's fixed-point witness.

grammar @epistemologic/math/lawvere {

  # the carriers ------------------------------------------------------
  # the grammar reasons about Lawvere structure without caring what the
  # carriers concretely are. mirror's eigenboard, the BEAM runtime, and
  # the Fate chip each instantiate the same shape with different state.

  type point            # a position in the space the endomap acts on.
                        # for the eigenboard: a section of the bundle.
                        # for an autopoietic system: a tick-state.
                        # for untyped lambda: a term.

  type endomap          # a structure-preserving self-map X → X.
                        # for the eigenboard: the kintsugi tick.
                        # for an autopoietic system: the self-production map.
                        # for untyped lambda: a function term.

  type fixed_point      # a point x such that endomap(x) = x.
                        # the generative zero, not absence zero.
                        # at the closure level of the bundle tower.
                        # the section that returns identity under the
                        # connection-induced action.

  type closure          # the autopoietic stable state.
                        # a grammar's Closure trait realizes this type.
                        # the system reproduces the conditions of its own
                        # reproduction. the structural ground.

  type self_reference   # the diagonal argument's structural object
                        # (Lawvere 1969). a function from X to X^X that
                        # exhibits the fixed-point property: if it is
                        # point-surjective, every endomap of X has a
                        # fixed point. Y combinator is the canonical
                        # instance for untyped lambda.

  # actions -----------------------------------------------------------

  # is_fixed_point: does x satisfy endomap(x) = x under the bundle's
  # connection-induced action? sub-Turing decidable for finite x and
  # finite endomap; for the eigenboard's 5-node bundle, the check is
  # one section comparison.
  abstract action is_fixed_point(x: point, f: endomap) -> verdict

  # is_autopoietic: does this grammar's tick → tick map have a Lawvere
  # fixed point? per Soto-Andrade & Varela 1984: a system is autopoietic
  # iff the self-production map has a Lawvere fixed point. the verdict
  # is the autopoietic closure verdict.
  abstract action is_autopoietic(grammar) -> verdict

  # diagonalize: construct the diagonal argument object (Lawvere 1969)
  # for a given endomap. the constructive heart of the theorem.
  # for the term algebra, this is Y f.
  abstract action diagonalize(f: endomap) -> self_reference

  # close: the closure-level action, lifted from @epistemologic/math/bundle.
  # returns the fixed point witnessing autopoietic closure.
  # this is the Closure trait of the bundle tower (level 4); the bundle
  # grammar's `close()` returns `fixed`, this grammar's `close()` returns
  # the same value typed as the Lawvere fixed point.
  abstract action close(f: endomap) -> fixed_point

  # the literal property ----------------------------------------------
  # does this implementation's Closure trait actually return a Lawvere
  # fixed point under measurement? the IS-relationship.
  # for the eigenboard: does close() return a section that the next
  # tick maps to itself?
  # for the compiler: does `mirror compile` of the bootstrap return a
  # crystal whose recompilation produces the same crystal?
  # mirror's epistemologic discipline measures the claim.
  property literal(implementation) -> verdict { \ }

  # the autopoietic property ------------------------------------------
  # does the grammar's tick → tick map have a Lawvere fixed point?
  # the bridge from Lawvere 1969 to Soto-Andrade & Varela 1984:
  # autopoietic IS having-a-Lawvere-fixed-point.
  property autopoietic(grammar) -> verdict { \ }

  # the fixed-point property ------------------------------------------
  # does the carrier X have the fixed-point property? per Lawvere 1969:
  # X has the fixed-point property iff there is a point-surjective map
  # X → X^X. the constructive condition for autopoiesis.
  property has_fixed_point_property(carrier) -> verdict { \ }
}

out point
out endomap
out fixed_point
out closure
out self_reference
out is_fixed_point
out is_autopoietic
out diagonalize
out close
out literal
out autopoietic
out has_fixed_point_property
out @epistemologic/math/lawvere
